text:
I have a little house in England;
It is small but it has its place;
And I love that little house of mine
As if all else were not at peace.

The roof is thatched with straw so brown,
So brown as autumn leaves are blown;
There's no chimney to be seen or heard,
But there's a fire inside my heart.

THE BURR

The Burr was a man of many parts;
He had his hand in every art;
And he could play on flute or fiddle,
Or sing with voice like to a bird.

But when it came to writing poetry,
His verses were not very good;
For though they rhymed as well as any,
They never made one think at all.

THE HOUSE OF LIFE

I HAVE a house of many rooms;
The walls are made of books;
And in each room there is a flame
That burns with love to look at them.

There’s one where I can read all day
Of men who lived before me;
Another where they tell me how
To live my own life better.

One holds the songs that make me glad,
A little chamber full of dreams;
While others hold the things I know
Which help me when I seem to fail.

THEY WERE ALL OF THEM

They were all of them--the men who had been in the room when he was born;
they were all there now, as they had always been there before, to see him go out into the world. They stood at his bedside, one by one, each with a gift or token from their hearts. The old man came first, bringing a silver cup filled with wine that had never known water. He said it would be good luck if she drank some on her wedding day. Then the young woman brought a gold ring set with an emerald, saying it

THE DEATH OF THE BALLAD

I HAVE a song I sing to myself;
It is an old ballad of my youth;
And when I hear it in the nightfall
The stars are all like eyes that watch me through.

My heart grows heavy with its tale
Of love and loss and bitter pain;
But still I know there’s something good
In every sorrow if we only strain.

The Dog

I have a little dog as brown as dough;
He's not so lively as he might be;
But when I take him out to play with me,
His tail is up like this (up goes his tail).

And then we run along together all day long,
Till at nightfall we come back home again;
